{"summary":"The core snapshot/rollback objective was demonstrated successfully before restart: `/srv/data` was on XFS/LVM, an earlier snapshot was verified, live data was changed, and merging the snapshot restored the recorded files, hashes, ownership, permissions, and directory structure. A verified snapshot of the restored state was also retained. The attempt became blocked only after scheduling a reboot: node1 stopped accepting commands and never returned during polling. The evidence cannot establish whether the guest failed to boot, booted without management connectivity, or was affected by a provider-side reachability problem. Persistence, post-reboot OS/kernel identity, and post-reboot snapshot health therefore remain unproven.","evidence":[{"source":"Pre-snapshot audit","finding":"`/srv/data` was an XFS filesystem on `/dev/mapper/vg_appdata-data`; the three baseline files, SHA-256 hashes, modes, owners, and sizes were recorded. Replacement swap and sshd were active with no failed units."},{"source":"Snapshot creation and direct mount","finding":"`data_snap_before_change` was created while XFS was frozen, mounted read-only with `nouuid`, and contained the same recorded tree and hashes as the baseline."},{"source":"Mutation audit","finding":"`application.conf` content and mode changed, `ledger.txt` was deleted, and `post-snapshot.txt` was added, proving modification occurred after the snapshot."},{"source":"Merge and restoration audit","finding":"After snapshot merge and remount, the original hashes, paths, modes, ownership, and sizes returned; the deleted ledger reappeared and the added file disappeared."},{"source":"Retained-snapshot audit","finding":"`data_snap_restored` existed as a 512 MiB snapshot and its direct read-only mount produced hashes matching the restored data."},{"source":"Restart audit","finding":"A transient systemd timer acknowledged one reboot. A check immediately afterward still showed the original boot ID; later checks timed out. No changed boot ID or post-restart health output was captured."},{"source":"Evidence limitation","finding":"The displayed manifest did not compare timestamps, ACLs, extended attributes, symlink targets, or SELinux contexts. Block-level snapshot merge strongly supports restoration, but the supplied direct proof of “exact metadata” is incomplete."}],"likely_causes":[{"cause":"The immediate blocker was failure of the execution/management path to return after the scheduled reboot; the evidence does not identify whether the failure was inside the guest or in provider connectivity.","confidence":"high"},{"cause":"The new persistent storage configuration may have delayed or blocked boot—for example, an incorrect `/etc/fstab` entry, failure to activate `vg_appdata`, or stale references to the repurposed swap partition. The relevant configuration and boot logs are absent, so this remains a hypothesis.","confidence":"medium"},{"cause":"A provider or management-plane reachability failure may have occurred even if the guest booted normally; command timeouts alone cannot distinguish this from a guest boot failure.","confidence":"medium"},{"cause":"XFS corruption or an incomplete snapshot merge caused the restart failure.","confidence":"low"}],"next_attempt_guidance":["Before changing storage, record `lsblk -f`, `blkid`, LVM state, `/etc/fstab`, `/proc/cmdline`, and any swap/resume references. Prefer unused storage over repurposing the system's existing swap partition.","If swap must be repurposed, replace every persistent old-swap reference, check for a stale resume UUID, and regenerate boot artifacts if the platform requires it.","Mount `/srv/data` using a stable UUID or validated LVM path. Before reboot, run `findmnt --verify --verbose`, exercise `mount -a`, verify `swapon --show`, confirm the VG/LVs are active, reload systemd configuration, and ensure no failed units exist.","Quiesce the application and freeze XFS only for snapshot creation. Directly mount the snapshot read-only with